# Environment template for the extracted Catalog service.
#
# This file declares the name of every environment variable the service reads and carries no values.
# Nothing in this subtree loads a `.env` file — there is no `dotenv` dependency and no loader, because
# `src/config/env.ts` reads `process.env` and nothing else — so creating a `.env` changes nothing on its
# own. The variables must be present in the process environment of whatever runs the service.
#
#   1. Locally. Do not have a shell read the filled-in copy as a program: a value would then be code
#      rather than data (CWE-78). Sourcing executes the values, so a password containing a command
#      substitution, a backtick, a semicolon, `&&` or a pipe is run rather than assigned — and a
#      high-entropy password is exactly the kind of string that contains those characters. Chaining the
#      shell's allexport option compounds it, because turning allexport back off then depends on the load
#      succeeding, and any failure strands the shell exporting every later assignment. Use a parser that
#      treats the file as data:
#
#          node --env-file=.env …
#
#      `--env-file` is built into the Node 20 line this service targets: Node parses the file itself, so
#      no shell interprets the contents and the calling shell is left unmodified. For a non-Node consumer
#      the requirement generalises — parse the file, never execute it.
#
#      Name the local copy exactly `.env`. .gitignore lists that literal filename rather than a wildcard,
#      deliberately, so this template stays committable — which means `.env` is ignored while
#      `.env.local`, `.env.dev` and `.env.production` are not, and a copy under any of those names is a
#      credential file git will happily stage. Check with `git check-ignore -q .env && echo ignored`
#      before writing a secret into anything.
#
#   2. In a deployment, where this file is not read at run time at all. Lambda function configuration, a
#      task definition, a Kubernetes secret or a CI variable group each set the process environment
#      directly, and this template is then the checklist of names to populate there. Infrastructure as
#      code is out of scope (AAP §0.2.2.5), so no such artifact is authored here.
#
# No credential is hardcoded anywhere in this port and none is committed here. Nothing below is needed to
# build, type-check, lint or test the subtree: `src/config/env.ts` validates the connection facts without
# opening a connection, the Google integration is a stub that makes no network call, and the suites are
# unit tests. A value is required only where a real MySQL connection is opened.
#
# Runtime lifecycle (CWE-1104): this service targets Node.js 20 and the Lambda `nodejs20.x` managed
# runtime, and that line is out of support. README.md §2.2 carries the one dated account of the schedule;
# it is not repeated here, because two copies of a moving forecast drift apart. The pin is deliberate and
# cited — AAP §0.5.5 resolves the evidence with "The pin stands." and AAP §0.5.3.2 rejects a newer
# @types/node on the ground that definitions must track the runtime. Filling this file in is therefore not
# by itself clearance to ship.

# Where these variables are read
# `src/config/env.ts` is the sole reader of the process environment in this subtree. Configuration then
# flows one way, in three branches that share a reader rather than a path:
#
#   1. environment -> src/config/env.ts -> src/config/database.ts -> the module-scope mysql2 pool
#      (the nine connection facts; nothing else reads them)
#   2. environment -> src/config/env.ts -> src/config/container.ts -> src/handlers/googleFeedHandler.ts
#      (GOOGLE_FEED_HOST, reached as `container.config.googleFeed` rather than by a direct import, which
#      is what makes it substitutable in a test without touching the environment)
#   3. environment -> src/config/env.ts -> src/config/container.ts -> StaticSettingResolver
#      (the three SETTING_* values, whose legacy default is computed at run time rather than stored)
#
# Only branch 1 passes through `src/config/database.ts`; the pool never sees the feed host or the setting
# values. Every layer below the config layer receives what it needs by constructor injection.
#
# The list below is exactly the key set `src/config/env.ts` reads: nineteen names, six required and
# thirteen optional.
#
#     loader                       total  required                       optional
#     ---------------------------  -----  -----------------------------  ------------------------------
#     loadDatabaseConfig()             9  db_host, db_port, db_name,  5  DB_TLS_MODE,               4
#                                         DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD           DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT,
#                                                                        DB_QUEUE_LIMIT,
#                                                                        DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
#     loadGoogleFeedConfig()           1  GOOGLE_FEED_HOST            1  —                          0
#     loadSettingsConfig()             3  —                           0  the 3 SETTING_* names      3
#     loadResourceBoundsConfig()       6  —                           0  the 6 CATALOG_* bounds     6
#     ---------------------------  -----  -----------------------------  ------------------------------
#     total                           19                              6                            13
#
# Neither side of that split is maintained by hand: `src/config/env.ts` declares the inventory as data
# (ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE_CENSUS) and asserts its own arithmetic at module load, and the suite reads that
# census, compares it against every `process.env` access in the loader and against this file. A name added
# on one side only fails a test.
#
# Every optional name here is commented out and every required one is present with an empty value, and
# that is not cosmetic: `src/config/env.ts` rejects a blank optional value, so an optional name left
# present-but-empty fails the load instead of applying the documented absent-behaviour. Required means
# present with no default substituted; absence of any of the six is fatal at load, with an error naming
# the variable. Absent behaviour differs per optional name:
#
#     DB_TLS_MODE             absent -> the verified transport mode; only a loopback DB_HOST may lower it
#     DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT     absent -> the option is omitted from the pool entirely, so the driver's own
#     DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS   documented behaviour governs; this port states no figure
#     DB_QUEUE_LIMIT          absent -> the lowest permitted bound, 1, because the driver reads an unset
#                             queue limit as unlimited and selecting unlimited silently is the one thing
#                             this fallback exists to prevent. It is a floor, not a capacity estimate
#     the three SETTING_*     absent -> that one setting name raises a classified configuration error when
#                             resolved, and every other name keeps working
#     the six CATALOG_*       absent -> the route that needs the bound refuses, with an error naming the
#                             variable to set. Loading succeeds with all six absent, which is what keeps
#                             the build, the linter and the suite runnable with no environment — but an
#                             absent bound is never read as "unbounded"
#
# Deliberately absent, because for these the driver defers a choice rather than making a poor one: no idle
# timeout, no maximum-idle count, no keep-alive interval, no statement bound, no reset-on-release setting
# and no connection-lifetime value. No ceiling is validated for any number either, because a ceiling would
# be a capacity figure with no source (IR-12).

# Provenance — the legacy application recorded only four connection-related values in source
# It named a ColdFusion/Railo datasource and let the application server resolve it out of band:
#
#   config/configApplication.cfm:L2  `<cfset this.datasource.name = "Slatwall" />`
#   Application.cfc:L78              publishes `datasource`         <- this.datasource.name
#   Application.cfc:L81              publishes `datasourceUsername` <- this.datasource.username
#   Application.cfc:L84              publishes `datasourcePassword` <- this.datasource.password
#   Application.cfc:L87              publishes `databaseType`       <- this.ormSettings.dialect
#   org/Hibachi/Hibachi.cfc:L12-L13  set username and password to the empty string
#
# Because the credentials are set to `""` there is no legacy credential to carry across, which is why
# every value below is blank rather than seeded.
#
# "Slatwall" is a datasource name: an alias registered in the application server and resolved out of band
# to a JDBC URL, a schema, a user and a password. It is never used as a schema, host, port or user, and
# admin/views/main/nodatasource.cfm:L12 confirms the indirection. DB_HOST, DB_PORT and DB_NAME are new
# here by necessity and are left blank rather than guessed.
#
# `config/configORM.cfm:L3-L14` chose the dialect at run time, probing the server and branching on the
# reported product name with no else-branch, so an unrecognised product left the dialect unset. This port
# targets MySQL only and resolves that branch at design time, so there is no DB_DIALECT and no
# DATABASE_TYPE variable here and none is read anywhere in `src/**`. No database engine version is
# asserted either, because the legacy repository pins none.

# Two shapes appear below, and the difference is the required/optional split:
#
#   NAME=            a required name, present with an empty value. Leaving it empty fails the load,
#                    naming the variable (except DB_USER and DB_PASSWORD, where an empty string is a real
#                    legacy value — see their entry).
#   # NAME=          an optional name, commented out. Absent selects the fallback documented beside it; a
#                    bare `NAME=` does not, because a blank optional value is rejected.
#
# The one exception to "no value is committed" is `# DB_TLS_MODE=verified`, which is commented out and
# carries the fallback the loader already applies, so uncommenting it changes nothing until the value is
# changed. No number, host, credential or identifier appears anywhere in this file.

# DB_PORT must be a plain base-ten TCP port number between 1 and 65535.
DB_HOST=
DB_PORT=

# Schema holding the existing Sw* tables. Required. This service reads and writes that schema and
# neither creates nor migrates it. Not the datasource name from config/configApplication.cfm:L2.
#
# Length is bounded at 64 characters, MySQL's documented maximum for a database identifier. A longer
# value cannot name a schema on any server, so it is rejected at load — naming this variable — rather
# than deferred to the first query, where the driver would report an unknown-database error naming
# neither the variable nor the environment. The bound is the server's, not a capacity figure chosen here.
# No character screening is applied: MySQL permits a wide range in a quoted identifier and the schema is
# the fixed contract both systems share, so rejecting a name that genuinely exists would be worse.
DB_NAME=

# Credentials, required, mapped from `this.datasource.username` and `this.datasource.password`
# (Application.cfc:L81, Application.cfc:L84). No value is committed here.
#
# These two are the only variables that may legitimately stay empty at run time: they must be present but
# may be the empty string, which reproduces the legacy `""` at org/Hibachi/Hibachi.cfc:L12-L13 exactly.
# Empty and absent are therefore different states, and only absent is an error.
#
# That tolerance exists for source parity and is not a recommended production posture. In any real
# deployment supply an explicit account scoped to least privilege on the Sw* schema, unless the database
# is intentionally and securely configured for credentialless access.
DB_USER=
DB_PASSWORD=

# How the connection is protected in transit. Optional — when unset the mode is `verified`, which is the
# fail-safe direction: absence encrypts, and the unencrypted mode has to be asked for by name. Exactly
# one of two tokens, matched case-sensitively:
#
#   verified   TLS is required, the server's certificate chain is verified against the runtime's trust
#              store, and the certificate is checked against the host connected to. The only accepted
#              value for any host that is not a loopback literal.
#   disabled   No TLS. Accepted only when DB_HOST is a loopback literal — `localhost`, an address in
#              127.0.0.0/8, or the IPv6 loopback. Its purpose is the local development database, whose
#              self-signed certificate no public trust store can verify. Setting it for a remote host is
#              rejected at load, not warned about.
#
# There is deliberately no third token, and none that keeps TLS while skipping verification: an
# unverified session is indistinguishable from an intercepted one.
#
# A private certificate authority needs no variable here and no code change. Supply the root to the Node
# runtime through its own documented extra-CA mechanism and keep this set to `verified`;
# src/config/database.ts reads no certificate file and parses no certificate.
#
# Commented out, like every optional name here. A bare `DB_TLS_MODE=` fails the load rather than falling
# back, because a name typed and left empty would look like working configuration while behaving as
# though nothing had been supplied. Uncomment it only to ask for `disabled` against a loopback database.
# DB_TLS_MODE=verified

# Resource bounds for the pool, all three optional. A value that is supplied must be a plain base-ten
# integer of at least one; none has a ceiling, because a ceiling would be a capacity figure with no
# source. Blank is a misconfiguration rather than a way to say "unset", so all three are commented out
# and none carries an example value — a committed example would be this port asserting a capacity figure
# the source never states (IR-12). AAP §0.3.2 records a single connection as the shape usually recommended
# for a serverless caller, but records it as engineering rationale and never as a figure.
#
# DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT is the greatest number of connections the pool may open. Unset -> the option is
# omitted and the driver's own bounded default applies.
#
# DB_QUEUE_LIMIT is the greatest number of connection requests that may wait once the connection limit is
# reached; beyond it a request fails fast instead of queueing without bound. Unset -> 1, the floor above.
# This is the one bound not delegated to the driver, and the reason is the substance of the setting: the
# driver reads zero as "no limit" and zero is its default, so omitting the option would select the
# unbounded queue this value exists to prevent. Zero is rejected when supplied, for the same reason.
#
# DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS is how long the driver may spend establishing a connection before it fails.
# Unset -> the option is omitted and the driver's own documented connect timeout applies. It bounds
# connection setup only; it is not a statement, request or invocation timeout, and it is not a latency
# target of any kind.
# DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT=
# DB_QUEUE_LIMIT=
# DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=

# Google product feed — the one name that is not a connection fact
# The host authority every absolute URL in the Google merchant feed is built on: the replacement for the
# legacy `CGI.HTTP_HOST` reads at integrationServices/google/views/feed/product.cfm:L14, :L15, :L22, :L23
# and :L24.
#
# It is configuration rather than a request header because the legacy took the value straight from the
# request, which on any platform means the caller chooses it. Three routes open as a result: markup in the
# header lands inside element text nodes and can add or replace feed fields; every product, image and
# additional-image URL in the document is rebased onto whatever authority the caller names; and one bare
# ampersand leaves the document with no defined XML parse at all. src/config/env.ts records the full
# reasoning as its Decision F.
#
# Form: a bare host authority — `store.example.com`, `store.example.com:8080`, an IPv4 literal, or a
# bracketed IPv6 literal — optionally followed by `:` and a port. No scheme: the `http://` prefix is
# hardcoded in the builder because the legacy hardcodes it.
#
# The value is judged in three places, none of which trusts the others to have run:
#
#   1. src/config/env.ts, at module load, requires it to be present and non-blank and to match the full
#      RFC 3986 section 3.2.2 `host` production with section 3.2.3's optional `port`. A value outside
#      that production raises a ConfigurationError naming GOOGLE_FEED_HOST, so a misconfiguration is a
#      cold-start failure rather than a bad document.
#   2. src/handlers/googleFeedHandler.ts re-applies an authority-delimiter deny check when the feed's
#      collaborators are constructed, so a container built from a hand-supplied value is judged too.
#   3. src/integrations/google/ProductFeedBuilder.ts re-applies it per render, atomically, before any byte
#      is produced.
#
# The grammar is `host [ ":" port ]`, where `host` is one of: a registered name (ASCII letters, digits and
# `- . _ ~ ! $ & ' ( ) * + , ; =`, or a `%XX` percent-encoded octet — which subsumes an IPv4 literal such
# as `203.0.113.7`); a bracketed IPv6 literal such as `[2001:db8::1]`, which must also parse as a real
# IPv6 address; or a bracketed IPvFuture literal such as `[v1.fe80::a+en1]`. A `port`, when present, is
# held to exactly the rule DB_PORT is held to.
#
# Rejected by name at load, each raising rather than reaching the feed:
#   - a scheme prefix, e.g. `http://store.example.com`. The scheme is not yours to set here.
#   - userinfo, e.g. `user@store.example.com`. `CGI.HTTP_HOST` could never carry it — RFC 9110 section
#     7.2 excludes it from the Host field — and admitting it would let `store.example.com@evil.example`
#     move the origin of every URL in the document (CWE-601).
#   - a path, query or fragment: `store.example.com/feed`, `store.example.com?a=1`,
#     `store.example.com#top`. A `#` would otherwise collapse all five URLs onto one page.
#   - a backslash, whitespace anywhere, and any ASCII control character.
#   - a bare, unbracketed IPv6 address such as `2001:db8::1`. Bracket it.
#   - any non-ASCII character. Supply an internationalised name as its A-label (`xn--…`), which is the
#     form that reaches the wire regardless.
#   - a blank value. Blank is not a way to say "unset", and this name is required.
#
# The diagnostic names the variable and never echoes the value, because a rejected authority is
# attacker-supplied text by hypothesis.
#
# Two things are decided nowhere, so the remaining exposure reads as a decision:
#   - The identity of the host. Nothing checks whether the authority you name is one you control. There
#     is no allowlist of permitted feed hosts and no DNS or reachability probe: an allowlist would reject
#     authorities a conforming deployment may legitimately name, and neither list exists in the legacy
#     source to carry across.
#   - Normalisation. A value that passes is published verbatim. It is not trimmed, case-folded,
#     punycoded, bracketed, stripped of a default port or upgraded to a secure scheme.
#
# The importer's import-source policy is a different thing, and the two look alike enough to be worth
# separating. This value is a host the operator states once and the feed writes into output. The
# importer's location arrives from a caller and is dereferenced server-side, which is a different threat
# with a different owner: it is decided by ProductImportSourcePolicy, an injected collaborator declared in
# src/ports/repositories/ProductRepository.ts, and the composition root's default reader refuses
# fail-closed until a deployment supplies one.
#
# Required, and therefore present-and-blank here. There is no host to inherit from legacy source, so no
# value is committed and none is defaulted (IR-12).
GOOGLE_FEED_HOST=

# Setting values — the three of eighteen whose legacy default is computed, not stored
# Three optional names, read by one collaborator below the config layer:
# `src/adapters/settings/StaticSettingResolver.ts`. Its `StaticSettingResolverConfiguration` argument is
# optional and defaults to `{}`, so a deployment that states none of the three still resolves every
# setting name that comes from a frozen literal. They are read here rather than there because
# src/config/env.ts is the sole reader of the environment in this subtree, which it records as Decision G.
#
# Fifteen of the eighteen setting names this slice reads resolve from frozen literals and need nothing
# here; these three are computed at run time by the legacy setting service, two of them from services this
# slice excludes, so no static table can hold them. Supplying one makes the corresponding setting name
# resolvable; leaving one unset leaves that one name — and only that name — raising a classified
# configuration error, while every other name continues to work. A deployment that reads only
# `globalDateFormat` must not be forced to invent currency and fulfillment lists it has no business
# knowing.
#
# Leave them unset rather than empty: a name typed and left empty is rejected at load, because a blank
# path resolves to a bare suffix and a blank identifier list is a list of nothing. (Note the deliberate
# contrast with DB_USER and DB_PASSWORD, where blank is a real legacy value and absent is the error.)
#
# SETTING_APPLICATION_ROOT_MAPPING_PATH is the CFML application scope's `applicationRootMappingPath`,
# from which model/service/SettingService.cfc:L164 derives `globalAssetsImageFolderPath`. Supply the path
# only, without the `/custom/assets/images` suffix — the suffix is the legacy's and the setting adapter
# appends it, so a path that already includes it produces a doubled suffix.
#
# SETTING_SKU_ELIGIBLE_CURRENCIES is the comma-delimited currency identifier list
# model/service/SettingService.cfc:L222 computes as `getCurrencyService().getAllActiveCurrencyIDList()`,
# for example `USD,CAD`. A delimited string rather than separate names because the legacy consumer reads
# it with list functions (model/entity/Sku.cfc:L373 and :L375), and the Currency* family is out of scope.
#
# SETTING_SKU_ELIGIBLE_FULFILLMENT_METHODS is the comma-delimited fulfillment-method identifier list
# model/service/SettingService.cfc:L223 computes as
# `getFulfillmentService().getAllActiveFulfillmentMethodIDList()`. Two independent facts make it
# unanswerable statically: the computation reaches the excluded Fulfillment* family, and the three seeded
# rows at config/dbdata/SlatwallSetting.xml.cfm:L14-L16 are scoped by productTypeID while the port's
# resolution context carries an entity kind and identifier only.
# SETTING_APPLICATION_ROOT_MAPPING_PATH=
# SETTING_SKU_ELIGIBLE_CURRENCIES=
# SETTING_SKU_ELIGIBLE_FULFILLMENT_METHODS=

# Finite resource bounds — six names, and the service does not serve without them (CWE-400)
# Read this before deploying. A deployment that states none of these six has a service that refuses every
# request rather than one that runs unbounded, and that direction is deliberate: an absent bound is an
# absent decision, never a licence to run without one. The four exposures they close are unbounded SKU
# combination generation that could also fail to terminate, smart-list and feed work with no
# materialisation or complexity ceiling on any authenticated route, an unbounded per-record image count
# and response size on the anonymous feed route, and unbounded URL-title collision probing.
#
# Nothing is invented, and that constraint shapes all six. No source file authors a figure and no default
# exists anywhere (AAP §0.7.3 standard 9, IR-12). Each bound is carried by a resolver: an unstated figure
# produces a ConfigurationError naming the variable to set, raised from the route that needed it. So the
# refusal reports an absent decision; it never substitutes for one. Choosing the figures is the operator's
# job, because only the operator knows the catalog, the instance size and the invocation timeout they are
# deploying against.
#
# Which routes need which, so a partial configuration is diagnosable rather than mysterious:
#   CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_RECORDS_PER_QUERY      every smart-list read, which is every read route
#   CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_PREDICATES_PER_QUERY   every route (same), AND the three statements composed
#                                                 from a caller-supplied list outside the smart list:
#                                                 product.getProductSkusBySelectedOptions,
#                                                 option.getUnusedProductOptions and
#                                                 option.getUnusedProductOptionGroups
#   CATALOG_SKU_MAX_COMBINATIONS_PER_REQUEST      sku.createSkus, and product.saveProduct through it
#   CATALOG_URL_TITLE_MAX_PROBES_PER_DERIVATION   product.saveProduct, product.saveProductType,
#                                                 brand.saveBrand
#   CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_IMAGES_PER_RECORD     google:feed.product
#   CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES        google:feed.product
#
# The anonymous feed route additionally refuses early, before it composes a query or resolves an image
# path, naming the first of its four figures that is unset. Every other route refuses at the moment the
# figure would have been applied.
#
# Leave an unused name unset rather than empty. A value that is supplied must be a plain base-ten integer
# of at least one; zero, a negative, a fraction, a blank and anything non-numeric are all rejected at
# load, naming the variable. Zero is the dangerous one to admit silently, since a bound of zero would
# refuse every request rather than bounding it. None has a ceiling, because a ceiling would be a capacity
# figure with no source.
#
# CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_RECORDS_PER_QUERY is the largest number of records one smart-list query may
# materialise. Applied by both execution members of the query builder — including executeRecords, the
# member the feed reads through, whose statement carries no LIMIT — by counting before hydrating and
# refusing an over-budget selection rather than truncating it. This one is a smart-list gate only: the
# three direct statements named below apply the complexity figure and no row ceiling, because their result
# cardinality comes from stored catalogue data rather than from anything a caller sends, and because one of
# them runs inside a save — a row ceiling there would make a product with more SKUs than the figure
# unsaveable. See README section 8.1.
#
# CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_PREDICATES_PER_QUERY is the largest number of query-complexity units one
# composed statement may carry, where a unit is one bound parameter plus one ORDER BY term plus one
# registered query source. This one figure bounds keyword cardinality (keywords × keyword properties),
# FI:/FIR: list cardinality (one placeholder per element), OrderBy cardinality and join count together —
# and it bounds statement size too, because no caller-supplied value ever reaches the emitted text: every
# one is bound through a `?` placeholder, so a megabyte-long keyword adds one unit and zero characters of
# SQL. Refused after composition and before execution.
#
# The same figure also bounds the three statements composed from a caller-supplied list OUTSIDE the smart
# list, which reach MySQL directly and so were never covered by the gate above:
#
#   product.getProductSkusBySelectedOptions   2N + 3 units (one correlated EXISTS per selected option,
#                                             duplicates included, plus the product predicate and the
#                                             option-bearing guard). Effective ceiling: (bound - 3) / 2.
#   option.getUnusedProductOptions           N + 7 units (N group identifiers plus the product
#                                             identifier, four sources, two ORDER BY terms).
#   option.getUnusedProductOptionGroups      N + 2 units (N group identifiers, one source, one ORDER BY
#                                             term).
#
# For those three the units are derived arithmetically from the list length and checked BEFORE any
# statement text is composed, so an over-budget request assembles nothing and binds nothing. The refusal
# rejects; it never trims the list, because a trimmed list answers a different question.
#
# CATALOG_SKU_MAX_COMBINATIONS_PER_REQUEST is the largest number of SKU combinations one merchandise
# createSkus request may enumerate — the odometer at model/service/SkuService.cfc:L58-L211. A value of 1 is
# meaningful rather than degenerate: :L67 starts `totalCombos` at 1, so a ceiling of 1 admits the
# option-less default SKU and refuses everything larger. Applied between the count and the first SKU
# allocation, so an over-budget request constructs, attaches and validates nothing. A combination product
# that is no longer an exact integer is refused regardless of this figure, because the legacy loop cannot
# terminate on such an input.
#
# CATALOG_URL_TITLE_MAX_PROBES_PER_DERIVATION is the maximum number of uniqueness probes one URL-title
# derivation may issue. It bounds the probe rather than the algorithm: the slug transformation and the
# `-2`-first suffix sequence of model/service/DataService.cfc:L53-L71 are unchanged for every derivation
# that stays in budget. It counts the pre-loop probe at :L62 as well as the loop's, so a ceiling of 1
# admits exactly the no-collision case.
#
# CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_IMAGES_PER_RECORD is the largest number of g:additional_image_link elements one
# feed record may emit. integrationServices/google/views/feed/product.cfm:L24 loops the product's whole
# image collection inside the per-SKU loop with no ceiling, and each image costs one image-path port call.
# Checked before the loop, so an over-budget record resolves no path at all.
#
# CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES is the largest size in bytes the rendered feed document may
# reach. Bytes rather than characters because the response is UTF-8 on the wire, where one character can
# be four bytes. Measured on the finished document, and refused rather than truncated — a truncated RSS
# document is malformed, not smaller.
# CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_RECORDS_PER_QUERY=
# CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_PREDICATES_PER_QUERY=
# CATALOG_SKU_MAX_COMBINATIONS_PER_REQUEST=
# CATALOG_URL_TITLE_MAX_PROBES_PER_DERIVATION=
# CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_IMAGES_PER_RECORD=
# CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES=
